<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    The Nocturne palette, for the parts of the app that are Android resources
    rather than Flutter: the home-screen widget, its setup screen and its
    scope picker.

    These are the *light* values; values-night/colors.xml holds the dark ones.
    The widget itself is dark-first like the app — its panel and text read from
    the `nocturne_*` tokens below, which do NOT flip with the system theme,
    because a widget sits on the user's wallpaper and has no app ground to
    follow. Only the setup screen, which is an ordinary Activity, is themed.

    Source of truth: lib/app/theme/app_colors.dart.
-->
<resources>
    <!-- ===== Nocturne, fixed. Used by the widget's own surfaces. ===== -->
    <color name="nocturne_surface">#232532</color>
    <color name="nocturne_ink">#292B31</color>
    <color name="nocturne_accent_900">#2B2741</color>
    <color name="nocturne_text">#F2F2F6</color>
    <!--
        The muted ramp, flattened to a near-white band.

        In the app these steps sit on a known dark ground, so 58% / 42% / 32%
        read fine. The widget does not: it has no panel by default (see
        `WidgetConfig.ground`) and sits directly on the user's wallpaper. The
        first fix raised the floor — 85% / 68% / 55% — but kept the app's wide
        spread, and the spread is the thing that does not survive: on an
        unknown photograph a step at 68% does not read as *secondary*, it
        reads as *smudged*, because the ground it is being judged against is
        not the one the step was picked for.

        So the steps below sit inside a 15-point band under full white, and
        the hierarchy is carried by size and weight instead — the same trick
        the platform's own transparent widgets use, where a 14sp label and a
        32sp value are both essentially white and never confusable. Measured
        over a mid-grey wallpaper with no panel, the old caption step read a
        clear grey against its title; these read as one family.

        The shadow in `nocturne_text_shadow` does the rest.
    -->
    <!-- text at 92% — the note step. -->
    <color name="nocturne_text_note">#EBF2F2F6</color>
    <!-- text at 89%, 87%, 85%, 72%: the muted ramp, flattened. -->
    <color name="nocturne_text_muted">#E3F2F2F6</color>
    <color name="nocturne_text_inactive">#DEF2F2F6</color>
    <color name="nocturne_text_caption">#D9F2F2F6</color>
    <color name="nocturne_text_unchecked">#B8F2F2F6</color>
    <color name="nocturne_accent">#A79BE8</color>
    <!-- accent-300: the accent at paragraph size. -->
    <color name="nocturne_ink_accent">#DEDBFF</color>
    <!-- accent at 65% — the scope chip's border. -->
    <color name="nocturne_accent_40">#A6A79BE8</color>
    <!-- Hairline: text at 28%. -->
    <color name="nocturne_divider">#47F2F2F6</color>

    <!--
        The shadow every piece of widget text carries.

        Light type on an unknown wallpaper is only ever as readable as its
        darkest neighbour, and a widget has no say in what is behind it. A
        near-black blur under each glyph gives one: on a dark wallpaper it is
        invisible, on a bright photo it is the only thing keeping the text
        from vanishing. Cheaper and far more robust than a scrim, which would
        fight the opacity slider the setup screen exposes.

        It is dense (90%) and *slightly* diffuse — 3dp at dy 1, applied in the
        layouts. The density is what carries a bright ground; the radius is
        what stops the result reading as an embossed outline rather than as
        white text. An earlier 2dp/dy 0.5 was tighter and drew a hard rim
        around each glyph; going the other way, 6dp at a lower alpha turns
        into a grey haze that is worse than no shadow at all. Both ends were
        rendered and compared — see docs/ANDROID_WIDGET.md.
    -->
    <color name="nocturne_text_shadow">#E6060711</color>

    <!-- The launch window's ground: the app's own `bg`. See
         drawable/launch_background.xml. -->
    <color name="launch_background">#161826</color>

    <!-- ===== Setup screen (an Activity, so it follows the device theme). ===== -->
    <color name="config_background">#E4E7F5</color>
    <color name="config_bar">#F3F5FE</color>
    <color name="config_text">#292B31</color>
    <color name="config_text_muted">#75798C</color>
    <color name="config_accent">#9184D9</color>
    <!-- Label colour on top of config_accent. Themed rather than a hardcoded
         white: the accent is the same in both themes, and the ink that reads
         on it is not. -->
    <color name="config_on_accent">#F3F5FE</color>
    <color name="config_divider">#CFD3E5</color>
    <!-- The setup screen's live preview sits on a wallpaper-ish ground. -->
    <color name="config_preview_backdrop_a">#B2B6CA</color>
    <color name="config_preview_backdrop_b">#9397AB</color>
    <!-- Unfilled part of a slider track. -->
    <color name="config_track">#CFD3E5</color>
</resources>
